- **`wrap`** is applied to each message first. A `nil` wrap is the identity; Pekko's `null` would fail (`TODO(TYP-14-nil-wrap)`, a conscious difference, Owner 2026-11-10).
- **Errors instead of exceptions (B).** A handler error or panic ends the replay and is returned (a panic as a `*actor.PanicError`); the replayed messages and the failed one are out of the buffer. The error is Pekko's `UnstashException` (`StashBufferImpl.scala:204-207, 271-286`), unexported: it carries the behavior that threw and the `Supervise` layers the replay crossed, and unwraps to the cause, so `ErrorIs` and `ErrorAs` match the cause (`Supervision.scala:74-76`). When the handler returns it, the actor installs that behavior before a supervisor acts: `PostStop` (default Stop), `PreRestart` (`Restart`) or the next message (`Resume`) reach it (`ActorAdapter.scala:152-160`; `Supervision.scala:174, 318, 327`). A failure of a start is not wrapped (`:193`).
- **Usage rule (D-6, pending since #889 S7c).** Call the buffer only from the actor's own turn (a handler, a `Setup` factory or the callback of `ForEach`/`Exists`) of the incarnation that built it; never from another goroutine, after `PostStop`, or with the buffer of an earlier incarnation after a restart (a restart reruns `Setup` and builds a new one). Replayed handlers get the context the buffer captured. Not enforced, as for `Context`; `TestUnstashAll_ReplayThroughTheCapturedContextOfAnEarlierTurn` pins a later turn replaying through it.
- **`Same`, `Unhandled` and layers (A2).** `Same` as the starting behavior is the behavior in progress of an enclosing `Unstash` (a handler that unstashes again) or the actor's current one; a buffer with no actor behind it has none and returns `ErrInvalidInitialBehavior`, as does a `Setup` that resolves to `Same` or `Unhandled` (`PORT-GAP(TYP-14-setup-same)`, `StashBufferImpl.scala:227-236`: Pekko resolves both). `Unhandled` as the start is `ErrUnstashUnhandled` (`StashBufferSpec.scala:261-265`). An `Unhandled` answer keeps the behavior and records the message like an unhandled message of the actor (not published yet, see "Unhandled"). A `Supervise` crossed by a start is installed with the final behavior, so a later failure restarts the original behavior.
- **`Stopped` ends the replay; the rest are dead letters (C).** A `Stopped` start or result is returned for the handler to return, and the messages not yet taken among the `n` are published once, in order, as `DeadLetter{Msg: wrap(msg), Sender: deadLetters, To: the stashing actor}` (`StashBufferImpl.scala:218-223, 238-243, 246-251`); those beyond `n` stay in the buffer, the interpreted ones are not published. A `DeadLetterSuppression` that `wrap` keeps is a `SuppressedDeadLetter`, an ordinary `DeadLetter` if `wrap` changes its type. A `Setup` returned by a handler that starts as `Stopped` ends the replay with the rest still in the buffer (`:195`). A buffer with no actor behind it only takes the messages. Observed in the Pekko oracle `unstash-deadletters-2026-11-10.md` (US1 [007], US2, US3 [005], US4 [008]).
  `PORT-GAP(TYP-14-signal-message)`: a message of the Go type `Signal` is replayed as a message. `PORT-GAP(TYP-14-backoff-1063)` (`StashSpec.scala:539-564`), `PORT-GAP(TYP-14-stop-strategy-1063)` (`:483-516`: the default Stop is tested instead) and `PORT-GAP(TYP-14-interceptor-978)` stay open.
- **Evidence (ADR 0033 D13):** read against `StashBufferImpl.scala` and `StashBufferSpec.scala` (v1.7.1), not run; no Typed parity is claimed. U8 (empty buffer) and the D-6 test have no Pekko test row: alternative evidence, pending the Owner's approval.

Flow of a replay (`Unstash`):

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  C[Unstash behavior, n, wrap] --> E{buffer empty?}
  E -->|yes| R0[return behavior, unstarted]
  E -->|no| S{start behavior}
  S -->|Unhandled| X[ErrUnstashUnhandled]
  S -->|Same| T{in progress, or the actor's current?}
  T -->|found| U
  T -->|no actor behind the buffer| Y[ErrInvalidInitialBehavior]
  S -->|other| U[start: Setups run, layers kept]
  U -->|Stopped start| D[rest of the n to dead letters, return Stopped]
  U -->|Setup resolves to Same or Unhandled| Y
  U --> M{messages left, n > 0?}
  M -->|no| R1[return the behavior in force]
  M -->|yes| H[wrap of the head to the behavior in force]
  H -->|handler error or panic| ER[return the error with the behavior that threw]
  H -->|Same, or Unhandled recorded| M
  H -->|Stopped| D2[rest of the n to dead letters, return Stopped]
  H -->|other| U
```

## Stopped (#1182 slices A and B, TYP-48)

Scope: [`docs/scopes/1182-typed-stopped.md`](../docs/scopes/1182-typed-stopped.md). Source: [`typed/behavior.go`](typed/behavior.go), [`typed/interpreter.go`](typed/interpreter.go). Tests: [`stopped_test.go`](typed/stopped_test.go). Runnable: [`ExampleStoppedWith`](typed/example_test.go).

`Stopped()` and `StoppedWith(postStop)` are `Behaviors.stopped` and `Behaviors.stopped(postStop)` (`Behaviors.scala:76, 90`). A handler, a `Setup` or the initial behavior that returns one ends the
actor through the hierarchy (`Context.StopSelf`: the children stop first). The behavior that was current gets the `PostStop` signal and then the optional callback runs **once**.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  R[handler, Setup, initial behavior or restart returns Stopped] --> S[StopSelf: children terminate first]
  S --> P[PostStop signal to the previous behavior, if any]
  P --> C[postStop callback, once]
  C --> T[watchers see a plain Terminated]
```

- **Implemented:** both constructors; the stop from a message handler, a `Setup`, the initial behavior and a restart that rebuilds into `Stopped`; signal then callback; the callback once even if `StopSelf` was also called.
  `Stopped` inside `Supervise(...)` with `Restart()` or `Resume()` is **not a failure**: no layer decides, the parent's decider is not consulted.
- **A `nil` callback is `Stopped()`** (Owner, 2026-11-10); Pekko would fail calling a null (`BehaviorImpl.scala:108-111`): `TODO(TYP-48-nil-callback)`. The callback takes no context, as Pekko's `() => Unit`.
  If the PostStop signal handler fails the callback does not run, as in Pekko.
- **Held inputs of a restart are dead letters (slice B).** When the replay of a restart meets a `Stopped` (a replayed handler returns it, or the new `Setup` is `Stopped`), every input still held goes to
  the dead-letter sink, in order, once: `DeadLetter{Msg, Sender: deadLetters, To: the stopping actor}` (`StashBufferImpl.scala:221, 241, 246-251`). A stop that arrives while the restart still waits discards them with no event.
  Tests: [`restart_stopped_test.go`](typed/restart_stopped_test.go), [`restart_stopped_wait_test.go`](typed/restart_stopped_wait_test.go). The actor reaches the sink through `actor.Context.System()`; `typed.Context` does not re-export it.
  - **Observed (Pekko 1.7.1, Java 8) in the restart's own stash** (`restart-stash-held-2026-11-11.md`): a held message is `DeadLetter(msg, deadLetters, stopping actor)`, in order, once (RS1 [019, 021]); a held `Terminated` is the typed signal as an **ordinary** `DeadLetter`, not a `SuppressedDeadLetter`, with the same sender and recipient, and the stopped actor never sees it (RS2 [017]; Go publishes `typed.Terminated`, because the classic `actor.Terminated` carries the suppression marker, #871). Count and order: oracle W3c (`typed-restart-child-wait-2026-11-06.md:66`).
  - **Not run in a restart's stash:** a new `Setup` that is itself `Stopped` (every held input is dead-lettered; it follows `StashBufferImpl.scala:241` and `unstash-deadletters-2026-11-10.md` US3 [005]); `ChildFailed`, `watchWith` and a full stash (`Dropped`).
- **Not here:** an interceptor around `Stopped`
  (`PORT-GAP(TYP-48-interceptor-978)`); the `Stop` variants of `TestNested_InnermostMatchWins` (`PORT-GAP(TYP-48-stop-variants-1063)`, the `Stop` strategy of #1063).
- **Evidence (ADR 0033 D13):** read against `Behaviors.scala`, `BehaviorImpl.scala`, `ActorAdapter.scala` and `StopSpec.scala` (v1.7.1), not run; Go scenarios on the shared hierarchy, pending the Owner's approval per row. No Typed parity is claimed.

